# Maven — the dominant build tool

A build tool automates compile → dependencies → test → package, the same on every machine. Maven has been the Java default for two decades.

## `pom.xml` — declarative

Describe the project in XML; Maven knows how to build it. The core is dependencies:

```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>com.google.guava</groupId>
    <artifactId>guava</artifactId>
    <version>33.0.0-jre</version>
</dependency>
```

## Coordinates + repository

Every library = **groupId · artifactId · version** (GAV). Maven fetches it from **Maven Central**, caches in `~/.m2`.

**Transitive dependencies** — ask for one library, get its dependencies too, whole tree resolved.

## Convention over configuration

`src/main/java` · `src/test/java` · `target/`. Follow the layout → almost no build logic; any dev knows the project.

## Lifecycle phases

```
validate → compile → test → package → install → deploy
```

`mvn package` = compile + test + jar (only if tests pass). Work done by **plugins**.

Trade-off: **verbose XML**, prefers its own way.
